# domain
- a Session is a parsed recording of an agent interaction, normalized from any supported source into a unified structure with events, stats, and metadata
- an Event is an atomic unit in a Session: user message, assistant response, or tool_result
- a Chapter is a sequence of Events between user prompts — one user request and its resolution
- a Source is the agent platform that produced a Session (claude, codex, cursor, factory, grok, opencode)
- the Waveform is a canvas chart where x=assistant turns, y=tokens (output above center, input below), color=primary tool, brightness=inverse cache ratio
- a Session contains Events; Events are grouped into Chapters by user message boundaries
- a Term is a lowercase token produced by the search tokenizer from Session content (word, camelCase/snake_case segment, or path segment)
- a Posting links a Term to a Session with the Term's frequency in that Session
- the Vocabulary is the sorted set of all unique Terms across indexed Sessions
- the SearchIndex is a compact inverted index over Session content: the Vocabulary, Postings per Term, and per-Session token counts (document lengths), stored separately from the metadata index
- the metadata index (index.json) stores per-Session metadata (firstPrompt, model, tools, tokens, errors, mtime, ...); full-text content belongs to the SearchIndex
- free-text search ranks Sessions by BM25 over the SearchIndex; fuzzy matching expands query Terms via Vocabulary trigram similarity
- a SessionFlyout is the right-hand inspector on the Runs home that shows the selected Session's rendered analytics without leaving the inventory @spec @session-flyout @implemented
- a RunAnalyticsPanel is the right-hand inspector on an opened chat/run that shows that run's existing /view analytics (grade, timeline, tiles, errors, charts, chapters) without leaving the conversation @spec @analytics-panel @implemented
- label: a CommandPalette is the app-wide Cmd/Ctrl+K overlay that searches Sessions and jumps to pages or in-view destinations
  id: cmdp
  tags: [spec, command-palette, implemented]

# share

## share > flow
- label: the share pipeline is: parse session → scan for secrets → redact findings in a session copy → render HTML from the clean copy → upload; secrets never leave the local machine
  command: test -f src/share/pipeline.js && test -f src/share/scanner.js && test -f src/share/redactor.js
  tags: [share, spec, implemented]
- label: redaction replaces each matched secret in every string-bearing field of the redacted session copy with [REDACTED] — including event.text, toolCalls[].input, thinking blocks, diffInfo, mcpInfo, and agentHistory records that renderHTML embeds; the redacted session is a deep copy — the original session data is never mutated
  command: grep -q '\[REDACTED\]' src/share/redactor.js
  tags: [share, spec, implemented]
- label: the share command is a new CLI subcommand alongside render, list, latest, find, search, messages, serve
  command: grep -qE 'case "share"' bin/tracequest.js
  tags: [share, spec, implemented]
- both GitHub Gist and Hugging Face Hub APIs support browser-origin requests via CORS (GitHub returns access-control-allow-origin: *; HF reflects the request Origin), so browser-side uploads work without a proxy @share @spec @implemented
- label: share redaction uses the shared redactValue tree walk for PEM blocks and matched secrets, with no separate PEM-only tree traversal
  command: grep -q 'redactPemBlocks(redactString' src/share/redactor.js && ! grep -q 'redactPemInTree' src/share/redactor.js
  tags: [share, share-redactor-anneal, spec, implemented]

## share > cli
- `tracequest share <session-path>` parses the session, runs the safety pipeline (scan → redact → render), uploads to the default target (gist), and prints the public URL @share @share-cli @spec @implemented
- `tracequest share <session-path> --target gist|hf` selects the upload target; default is gist @share @share-cli @spec @implemented
- `tracequest share --latest [project]` shares the most recent session, reusing cmdLatest resolution logic @share @share-cli @spec @implemented
- label: `tracequest share --filter <expression>` applies the standard filter expression; when multiple sessions match, the most recent is shared
  command: grep -q -- '--filter' src/cli/cli-commands.js
  tags: [share, share-cli, spec, cli-filter, implemented]
- the share command supports --open (launch URL in browser), --json (print full result for scripting), --private (secret gist / private HF repo), --force (skip secret confirmation), and --hf-repo (HF dataset repo) @share @share-cli @spec @implemented
- label: the share command is exported as cmdShare from src/cli/cli-commands.js; bin/tracequest.js dispatches via case "share"
  command: grep -q 'export async function cmdShare' src/cli/cli-commands.js
  tags: [share, share-cli, spec, implemented]
- label: share --help documents all flags, both targets, and shows examples
  command: node ./bin/tracequest.js share --help 2>&1 | grep -q -- '--force'
  tags: [share, share-cli, spec, implemented]

## share > metadata
- the share pipeline builds a metadata object by assembling parsed Session fields, session.stats, buildSessionChapters() output, and discovery/index metadata (same sources as sessionToApiObject): sessionId, sessionHash, source, model, project, firstPrompt (truncated to 200 chars, matching FIRST_PROMPT_MAX_LEN), duration, inputTokens/outputTokens/cacheTokens, toolCounts, chapterCount, grade (computeSessionGrade to match rendered HTML), timestamp (startTime) @share @share-meta @spec @implemented
- adapters use metadata to set titles, descriptions, and filenames on the remote platform; the rendered HTML is opaque to the metadata layer @share @share-meta @spec @implemented

## share > gist
- label: the gist adapter uploads the rendered HTML as a single-file GitHub Gist via POST /gists
  command: grep -q 'method: "POST"' src/share/gist-adapter.js && grep -q '/gists' src/share/gist-adapter.js
  tags: [share, share-gist, spec, implemented]
- the gist filename is tracequest-{source}-{sessionHash}.html; the description is "{source} session — {firstPrompt} ({model}, {formattedDuration})" @share @share-gist @spec @implemented
- gists are public by default; --private (CLI) or a toggle (modal) creates a secret gist @share @share-gist @spec @implemented
- CLI auth: GITHUB_TOKEN env var → `gh auth token` fallback → clear error; browser auth: token input field, persisted in localStorage under tracequest-gh-token @share @share-gist @spec @implemented
- re-sharing the same session creates a new gist (no update/dedup) @share @share-gist @spec @implemented
- uses native fetch, no external dependencies @share @share-gist @spec @implemented
- label: gist adapter reads GITHUB_API_URL (default https://api.github.com) so integration tests can point uploads at a local mock server
  command: grep -q 'GITHUB_API_URL' src/share/gist-adapter.js
  tags: [share, share-gist, spec, implemented]

## share > huggingface
- the HF adapter uploads the rendered HTML and a metadata JSON sidecar to a Hugging Face dataset repository @share @share-hf @spec @implemented
- the target repo defaults to `{username}/tracequest-sessions` where username is resolved via GET /api/whoami-v2 when --hf-repo is omitted; overridable via --hf-repo (CLI) or modal input (browser) @share @share-hf @spec @implemented
- the adapter creates the dataset repo on first use if it doesn't exist (type: "dataset") @share @share-hf @spec @implemented
- each HF upload writes sessions/{source}/{sessionHash}.html and sessions/{source}/{sessionHash}.json; re-sharing the same session overwrites both files @share @share-hf @spec @implemented
- the JSON sidecar includes all metadata fields plus: eventCount, errorCount (stats.errorCount), tools (Object.keys(stats.toolCounts)), filePaths (chapterFileKeys across chapters), gitBranch, and cost estimate (estimateParsedStatsCost) — making the dataset useful for agent behavior research without parsing HTML @share @share-hf @spec @implemented
- the adapter creates a `README.md` dataset card on first upload with YAML frontmatter (license, tags: [agent-traces, tracequest], task_categories: [text-generation]); subsequent uploads do not overwrite it @share @share-hf @spec @implemented
- CLI auth: HF_TOKEN env var → read ~/.cache/huggingface/token → clear error; browser auth: token input field, persisted in localStorage under tracequest-hf-token @share @share-hf @spec @implemented
- label: uses the HF Hub REST API (POST /api/repos/create, POST /api/datasets/{namespace}/{repo}/commit/{rev}; POST /api/datasets/{namespace}/{repo}/preupload/{rev} for large HTML) with native fetch, no external dependencies
  command: grep -q 'preupload' src/share/hf-adapter.js && grep -q '/api/repos/create' src/share/hf-adapter.js
  tags: [share, share-hf, spec, implemented]
- HF's HTML file preview renders the self-contained session viewable directly in the browser @share @share-hf @spec @implemented
- label: HF adapter reads HF_API_URL (default https://huggingface.co) so integration tests can point uploads at a local mock server
  command: grep -q 'HF_API_URL' src/share/hf-adapter.js
  tags: [share, share-hf, spec, implemented]

## share > browser-ui
- render-session-rules.js already defines CSS for .hf-btn, .hf-modal-overlay, .hf-modal, .hf-modal-title, .hf-modal-label, .hf-modal-input, .hf-modal-hint, .hf-modal-status (.hf-error, .hf-success), .hf-modal-actions, .hf-modal-share, .hf-modal-cancel; implementation must use these existing classes @share @share-ui @spec @implemented
- label: render-core-shell.js adds a "Share" button to the header-actions div (alongside Export, Print, Markdown); clicking opens a modal with a target selector (Gist / HF), target-specific inputs (token, repo name), a private toggle, and Share/Cancel actions
  command: grep -q "openShareModal" src/render/render-core-shell.js && grep -q "hf-btn" src/render/render-core-shell.js
  tags: [share, share-ui, spec, implemented]
- the modal JS and both upload adapters (gist + HF API calls) are inlined in the rendered HTML; no server-side proxy needed — both APIs accept browser-origin requests; the self-contained HTML constraint is preserved @share @share-ui @spec @implemented
- the session detail page in tracequest serve IS the renderHTML output, so the share button and modal are present in both standalone HTML files and the serve UI with no separate implementation @share @share-ui @spec @implemented
- the modal embeds the secret rules JSON and scanner logic; on share click it scans the embedded session data, redacts a deep copy, rebuilds standalone HTML via an inlined client-side renderHTML equivalent (same shell/CSS/bundle as server renderHTML over the redacted SESSION), and uploads that artifact — the visible page DOM is never modified @share @share-ui @spec @implemented
- tokens and repo names persist in localStorage so repeat shares are single-click @share @share-ui @spec @implemented
- during upload: button disabled + spinner, status area shows progress; on success: public URL as clickable link (hf-success class) + copy-to-clipboard; on error: message with hf-error class @share @share-ui @spec @implemented

## share > safety

### share > safety > rules
- label: secret detection uses the full gitleaks ruleset (MIT-licensed), vendored as a JSON file shipped inside the package; all upstream rules are included, no manual curation
  command: test -f data/secret-rules.json
  tags: [share, share-safety, spec, implemented]
- each rule preserves the gitleaks structure: id, description, regex, optional keywords (fast pre-filter), optional entropy (Shannon entropy threshold), optional secretGroup (capture group index for the actual secret), optional allowlist (regexes for known false positives) @share @share-safety @spec @implemented
- the scanner honors all fields: keywords skip the regex when absent, entropy is checked when present, secretGroup isolates the secret for redaction, allowlist matches suppress the finding @share @share-safety @spec @implemented
- label: scripts/update-secret-rules.js fetches the latest gitleaks config TOML from upstream, converts to JSON, and overwrites the vendored file (full replacement); the TOML parser is a devDependency — not shipped in the package; the script logs a summary of rules added/removed/changed
  command: test -f scripts/update-secret-rules.js
  tags: [share, share-safety, spec, implemented]

### share > safety > scanner
- the scanner walks every string-bearing field in each parsed Session event (text, toolCalls[].input, thinking[], diffInfo, mcpInfo.params, etc.) and any agentHistory records included in the upload payload, leveraging the structured event model (event type, tool name, chapter boundaries) @share @share-safety @spec @implemented
- the scanner applies the per-rule keywords pre-filter before the regex, and checks entropy when the rule specifies a threshold, so most rules skip most events cheaply @share @share-safety @spec @implemented
- each finding includes: ruleId, description, match (redacted to first 4 + last 4 chars), and location { chapterIndex, eventIndex, eventType, toolName }; findings are sorted by event order @share @share-safety @spec @implemented

### share > safety > ux
- when findings are non-empty: CLI prints each finding (rule description, redacted match, chapter/tool location) and requires --force or interactive y/n; browser modal shows findings in a warning panel with a confirm checkbox before enabling the Share button @share @share-safety @spec @implemented
- when findings are empty, upload proceeds without prompting @share @share-safety @spec @implemented

## cli
- share --filter and share --latest both accept an optional positional project pre-filter (same as list/latest) @spec @share-cli @cli-filter @implemented

## metadata
- buildShareMetadata must not call buildIndex with a partial session list; index meta comes from peekSession or read-only cache lookup @spec @share-meta @implemented
- label: share via --filter or --latest does not prune unrelated sessions from index.json or search.idx
  command: node --test test/share/metadata-index-prune.test.js
  tags: [spec, share-meta, implemented]

# search
- label: search > SearchIndex/BM25 is the full-text path for JS and Rust indexing; buildIndex strips any legacy per-session searchText from returned metadata and index.json, public API/session HTML omit searchText, and freshly parsed/search-stale sidecar output uses transient termFreqs instead of searchText
  id: ep7
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'legacy searchText|inserts termFreqs into SearchIndex|browserPage session data does not include searchText|strips searchText from page items and liveSessions' test/index-writers.test.js test/sidecar-gate.test.js test/api.test.js test/route-handlers-api.test.js && cd sidecar && cargo test test_build_index_keeps_term_freqs_for_stdout_but_cache_omits_them
  tags: [spec, search, implemented, search-output-contract]
- label: BM25 search extracts snippets for multiple matched terms in a Grok result with one chat_history.jsonl scan while preserving query-term snippet order
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'Grok multi-term snippets scan chat_history once' test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js
  tags: [spec, search, cli-perf, implemented]

## tokenizer
- label: search > the tokenizer lowercases input and splits on non-alphanumeric characters, camelCase boundaries, snake_case underscores, and path separators, so "indexWriters" and "src/index_writers.js" both yield the terms "index" and "writers"
  id: lvq
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'lowercases input|splits on non-alphanumeric|camelCase: indexWriters|path example from fact lvq' test/sessions/search-tokenizer.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented, search-tokenizer-contract]
- label: search > the tokenizer discards tokens shorter than 2 or longer than 32 characters
  id: nna
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'discards tokens shorter than 2 chars|discards tokens longer than 32 chars' test/sessions/search-tokenizer.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented, search-tokenizer-contract]
- label: search > indexing and query parsing use the same single tokenizer implementation; no duplicated tokenizer logic exists
  id: 85b
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'query parsing and JS indexers share search-tokenizer.js' test/sessions/search-tokenizer-source-contract.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented, search-tokenizer-contract]
- label: search > all 6 source indexers (claude, codex, cursor, factory, grok, opencode) feed the same content that previously populated searchText (user prompts, assistant text, thinking, tool-result text) through the tokenizer into a per-session term-frequency map
  id: d7k
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'source indexers tokenize source content into per-session termFreqs' test/sessions/search-tokenizer-source-contract.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented, search-tokenizer-contract]
- label: search > tokenizer input per session is capped at the same 500,000-character budget that previously bounded searchText, enforced identically in the JS indexers and the Rust sidecar termFreqs path, so per-session term maps stay bounded and JS/Rust Postings remain identical
  id: 7et
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'respects budget cap|source index chunk budget uses shared TOKENIZER_INPUT_CAP' test/sessions/search-tokenizer.test.js && cd sidecar && cargo test test_append_index_search_text_incremental_cap && cargo test test_tokenize_cap_at_500k
  tags: [spec, search, implemented, search-tokenizer-contract]

## index
- label: search > buildIndex maintains the SearchIndex incrementally: when a session mtime changes its old Postings are removed and its new term-frequency map is inserted; when metadata cache mtime still matches, buildIndex re-tokenizes that session only if it is absent from the SearchIndex or an existing search.idx failed to load
  id: msg
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'search.idx deleted|existing corrupt search.idx' test/index-writers.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented, spec, search-index-rebuild-facts]
- label: search > the SearchIndex is persisted to ~/.cache/tracequest/search.idx as a versioned compact file separate from index.json; if an existing search.idx cannot be loaded, buildIndex treats the SearchIndex as empty and re-reads mtime-matched sessions because index.json metadata has no term-frequency payload
  id: 21x
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'existing corrupt search.idx' test/index-writers.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented, spec, search-index-rebuild-facts]
- label: search > search.idx writes are deferred and debounced with the same mechanism as index.json writes; the in-memory SearchIndex is updated immediately
  id: v99
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'updates in-memory SearchIndex immediately and defers index.json and search.idx writes until debounce' test/index-writers.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented, search-index-output-contract]
- label: search > index.json entries contain no searchText field; index.json stores per-session metadata only
  id: yb5
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'buildIndex strips legacy searchText from JS metadata and index.json|sidecar-emitted termFreqs are stripped from index.json and persisted in search.idx' test/index-writers.test.js
  tags: [spec, search, implemented, search-index-output-contract]
- label: search > when a session no longer present on disk is pruned from the metadata cache, the same pass removes its Postings and document length from the SearchIndex, and Vocabulary terms with empty posting lists are dropped no later than the next search.idx persist, so document count, average document length, and IDF reflect only existing sessions
  id: 03w
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'removes sessions not in validPaths|deleted session pruned on all-cache-hit path' test/sessions/search-index.test.js test/index-writers.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented, search-index-output-contract]
- label: search > on the shared ~3000-session synthetic pipeline corpus, the serialized search.idx is smaller than the total byte length of the tokenizer-input text it indexes (the content that previously populated searchText)
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-name-pattern 'search.idx smaller than total tokenizer-input bytes' test/sessions/index-search-pipeline-perf.test.js
  tags: [spec, search, implemented]
- label: index.json and search.idx are written atomically via a temp file in the same directory then renameSync over the target, so a crash or kill mid-write never leaves a torn cache file
  id: g9o
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'index.json and search.idx writers use same-dir temp files before rename|no .tmp residue after flush and index.json is valid JSON' test/index-writers.test.js
  tags: [spec, search, implemented, search-index-output-contract]
- label: pending index writes are flushed on SIGINT and SIGTERM as well as normal process exit, so interrupting tracequest serve does not lose the built index; handlers are registered only when a write is pending and removed after flush; the process re-raises the signal with the default handler so exit codes are conventional (130 for SIGINT, 143 for SIGTERM)
  id: eli
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'pending write registers signal handlers and flushDeferredIndexWrites removes them|normal process exit flushes pending index.json and search.idx|SIGTERM flushes pending index.json and search.idx|SIGINT flushes pending index.json and search.idx' test/index-writers.test.js
  tags: [spec, search, implemented, search-index-output-contract]
- BM25 search candidate filtering and result materialization use one session path lookup so CLI search does not allocate duplicate per-session path containers before applying maxResults @spec @search @cli-perf @implemented

## ranking
- label: search > searchSessions ranks matching sessions with BM25 (k1=1.2, b=0.75) over the SearchIndex Postings and document lengths; the BM25 scores of matched terms are summed and results are returned in descending score order
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'BM25 constants stay at fact-backed values|BM25 ranks by summed term scores in descending order' test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented]
- label: search > a session is returned for a multi-term query only if every query term matches it, each term via exact, fuzzy-expanded, or final-term prefix matching
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'multi-term AND accepts exact plus fuzzy and final-prefix matches only when every token resolves|multi-term query requires all terms to match' test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented]
- label: search > a quoted free-text term (a phrase wrapped in quotes) is tokenized into its terms, requires all of them to match a session, and the session is included only after the exact phrase is verified by substring match against session content via the on-demand scan, preserving quoted-phrase exactness without storing positions
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'quoted phrase: session included only if exact phrase is in content|quoted phrase with no matching sessions returns empty' test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented]
- label: search > searchSessions accepts a caller-overridable maxResults (default 50) and returns at most that many sessions
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'default maxResults is 50 over more than 50 matches|returns at most maxResults sessions' test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented]
- label: search > match snippets are type-tagged (text, command, file, error) and the CLI and web search result shape is identical to the legacy substring-scan path
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'result object has path, source, project, file, mtime, id, prompt, model, matches fields|match snippets have type field' test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented]
- label: search > snippet extraction runs only for the returned top-ranked results, using the existing per-source on-demand scan (including the SQLite path for opencode) driven by the session's matched Terms (including fuzzy- and prefix-expanded Terms); a result with no literal Term occurrence falls back to a firstPrompt text snippet
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'snippet extraction scans only returned top-ranked results|fuzzy and prefix-expanded matched terms drive snippet extraction|missing literal term snippet falls back to firstPrompt' test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented]
- label: textSnippetFromLine decodes JSON string escapes in the extracted slice before returning; snippet text never contains raw \n, \t, \", or \\ two-character backslash sequences from JSONL encoding; a trailing lone backslash at a slice boundary is stripped
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'text snippet decodes|slice boundary: match adjacent' test/sessions/scan-queries.test.js
  tags: [spec, search, implemented]
- label: for phrase queries, snippet extraction places the first snippet slot on a scan for the full phrase string (context window around the phrase occurrence); remaining slots fall through to per-term scans as usual
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'phrase query: first snippet contains the verbatim phrase' test/sessions/scan-queries.test.js
  tags: [spec, search, implemented]
- label: when the query contains one or more quoted phrases, searchSessions iterates BM25-ranked candidates in score order and runs phrase verification before counting toward maxResults; it stops as soon as maxResults verified results are collected or all candidates are exhausted; total candidate scans per query are capped at max(200, maxResults*10) to bound work on large corpora; the non-phrase path is unchanged and incurs no extra scans
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'phrase verification runs before maxResults cut' test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js
  tags: [spec, search, implemented]
- label: a vocabulary term reachable from multiple query tokens (e.g. exact match plus prefix expansion) contributes its BM25 score to a document at most once, at its highest resolved weight; AND match sets still include it per query token
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'duplicate resolved vocabulary term contributes once at its highest weight' test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented]
- label: phrase snippets reuse the scan results collected during phrase verification; a verified phrase result does not trigger a second session-file scan for the same phrase
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'phrase snippets reuse verification scan results instead of rescanning the phrase' test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented]

## fuzzy
- label: search > a query term absent from the Vocabulary is expanded to at most 3 Vocabulary terms with trigram Jaccard similarity of at least 0.4, each contributing its BM25 score multiplied by that similarity, so an expanded term always scores strictly lower than the same term matched exactly
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'fuzzy absent query expands to a similar Vocabulary term|fuzzy expansion constants and score weighting stay fact-backed|fuzzy query after upsert finds new session one edit away from query term' test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented]
- label: search > the trigram structure is built over Vocabulary terms only, not over session content
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'trigram fuzzy expansion uses Vocabulary terms, not session file content' test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented]
- label: search > the final term of a query additionally matches Vocabulary terms by prefix, supporting search-as-you-type
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'partial word as final token matches sessions with the full word' test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented]
- label: prefix expansion (fact 3ns) applies to the final non-phrase query token even when an earlier query token deduplicates to the same token form
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'duplicate final token still receives prefix expansion' test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented]

## integration
- label: search > the cli search command and the web filter bar free-text terms both route through the BM25 searchSessions path, so ranking, fuzzy, and prefix behavior are identical across surfaces
  id: 0oy
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern "prefix expansion: expr=token matches same sessions as searchSessions\('token'\)|fuzzy expansion: expr=misspelling matches same sessions as searchSessions\('misspelling'\)|camelCase parity: 'indexWriters' searchSessions count equals filter context count" test/filter-expr.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented]
- label: search > structured filter keys (project:, source:, tool:, model:, grade:, errors:, size:, age:, live) and boolean operators are unaffected; BM25 applies only to free-text terms
  id: cw8
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'structured keys and booleans are evaluated outside BM25 context' test/filter-expr.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented]
- label: search > when search.idx is absent or an existing search.idx cannot be loaded, the buildIndex + searchSessions path repopulates the SearchIndex from session files; searchSessions serves results from the in-memory SearchIndex and never reads searchText blobs
  id: fp4
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'after buildIndex runs with no search.idx|search.idx deleted|existing corrupt search.idx' test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js test/index-writers.test.js
  tags: [search, implemented, search-index-rebuild-facts]
- label: search > free-text filter expressions use SearchIndex match sets for session content and do not require per-session searchText fields
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'free-text expr matches session content via SearchIndex|expr bare body-only-marker matches SearchIndex term sets before query scan' test/cli/cli-commands.test.js test/sessions/search-sessions.test.js
  tags: [search, search-filter-facts, implemented]

## perf
- label: search > BM25 searchSessions first hit stays under 5ms/op on the shared 3000-session indexed corpus
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-name-pattern 'searchSessions BM25 first-hit under 5ms' test/sessions/index-search-pipeline-perf.test.js
  tags: [spec, search, implemented]
- label: search > cold buildIndex of 3000 synthetic Claude sessions including SearchIndex construction stays under 1200ms (JS pipeline)
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-name-pattern 'buildIndex cold indexes 3000 sessions under 1200ms' test/sessions/index-search-pipeline-perf.test.js
  tags: [spec, search, implemented]

# cli
- label: the CLI selects behavior by first-position subcommands rather than flat mode flags; top-level --list, --latest, and --serve are rejected as unknown options
  command: node ./bin/tracequest.js --help | grep -q 'tracequest <command>' && for opt in --list --latest --serve; do node ./bin/tracequest.js "$opt" >/tmp/tracequest-cli-baseline.out 2>&1; test "$?" -ne 0 && grep -q 'Unknown option' /tmp/tracequest-cli-baseline.out || exit 1; done
  tags: [cli-baseline, implemented]
- label: tracequest render <session> and tracequest latest [project] share --out, --preset, and --open render-output options; latest can also narrow or list recent sessions with --filter, --sort, and --limit
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cli-commands cmdRender|cli-commands cmdLatest|cmdLatest --filter|cmdLatest --limit' test/cli/cli-commands.test.js
  tags: [cli-baseline, implemented]
- label: tracequest serve starts the session browser on port 7777 by default; --port/-p or a built-in preset can choose another port, and --filter/-f applies the project filter before serving
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cli-commands cmdServe' test/cli/cli-commands.test.js
  tags: [cli-baseline, implemented]
- label: built-in presets are listed by tracequest presets or tracequest preset; --preset <name> is an option on render, latest, and serve rather than a top-level flat mode
  command: node ./bin/tracequest.js presets | grep -q 'Built-in presets' && node ./bin/tracequest.js preset | grep -q 'Built-in presets' && node ./bin/tracequest.js --help | grep -q -- '--preset <name>'
  tags: [cli-baseline, implemented]
- presets live in a presets/ directory at the package root as .yaml files; only built-in presets shipped with the package are usable by name (no arbitrary filesystem paths) @implemented
- a minimal built-in default preset (or the current hardcoded defaults) is represented in presets/ so that built-in configuration has an explicit home @implemented
- The CLI uses node:util.parseArgs with command-specific option schemas; unknown options fail with usage guidance instead of being silently ignored @implemented
- For tracequest serve, the port from --port/-p or a preset must be validated as integer 1-65535 before calling serve(); invalid (NaN, 0, 99999+) produces clear 'Invalid port ...' error + exit 1 instead of a node:net RangeError stack @implemented
- In the render branch, CLI rejects top-level agent directories (e.g. ~/.grok/sessions or ~/.claude/projects passed as direct positional) with clear error + exit 1 (dir vs file guard for roots); also wraps writeFileSync for --out in try/catch so ENOENT/permission/etc yields friendly 'Failed to write output to <path>: <msg>' + exit 1 (no uncaught stack) @implemented
- The serve subcommand accepts --filter/-f as its project filter, logs whether matching sessions were found before starting the server, and passes the filter to serve(port, filter) for the initial browser index @implemented
- CLI help is maintained in the shared src/cli/cli-help.js template, while bin/tracequest.js uses matching parseArgs schemas for command validation; CLI reference/help parity tests cover the documented option groups @implemented
- cli > the list command supports --filter <expression> flag that applies the same filter expression as the web app (project:, source:, tool:, model:, grade:, errors:, size:, age:, live, free text, AND/OR/NOT, parentheses) to filter discovered sessions @spec @cli-filter @implemented
- cli > the latest command supports --filter <expression> flag that applies the same filter expression as the web app to pick the most recent matching session @spec @cli-filter @implemented
- cli > the list and latest commands support --sort <key> flag (recent, duration, cost, tokens, errors, files, commits, chapters, grade) to sort results using sortSessionList @spec @cli-filter @implemented
- cli > when --filter is used in list/latest, sessions are converted to API objects (via buildIndex + sessionToApiObject + peekSession) so that the filter expression evaluator has access to all indexed fields (model, tools, tokens, errors, etc.) @spec @cli-filter @implemented
- the find command accepts optional positional and --filter filter expressions (same syntax as web app: project:, source:, tool:, model:, grade:, errors:, size:, age:, live, free text, AND/OR/NOT, parentheses) and prints matching sessions; without any filter expression it lists the 10 most recent sessions sorted by recency @spec @cli-find @cli-filter @implemented
- the find command supports --limit N (default 10 without any filter expression, default 20 with positional or --filter expression) to cap displayed results and --sort <key> (recent, duration, cost, tokens, errors, files, commits, chapters, grade) to order them when a filter expression is used @spec @cli-find @implemented
- the find command uses applyExprFilter when a positional or --filter expression is provided (which calls buildIndex, sessionToApiObject, parseFilterExpr, evalFilterExpr, sortSessionList) so filter behavior is identical to list --filter; without any filter expression it reuses findSessions recency order and ignores --sort (unlike list) @spec @cli-find @cli-filter @implemented
- cli > the find command is exported as cmdFind from src/cli/cli-commands.js and re-exported from src/cli/index.js; bin/tracequest.js dispatches to it via case 'find' @spec @cli-find @implemented
- the find command accepts a positional filter expression and evaluates it with the same filter-expression syntax as --filter @spec @cli-find @cli-filter @implemented
- cli > the search command takes a query string as positional argument (required) and performs full-text search across all stored sessions using searchSessions from scan-queries.js @spec @cli-search @implemented
- cli > the search command supports --limit N (default 20) to cap displayed results @spec @cli-search @implemented
- cli > the search command is exported as cmdSearch from src/cli/cli-commands.js and re-exported from src/cli/index.js; bin/tracequest.js dispatches to it via case "search" @spec @cli-search @implemented
- cli > the search command prints results showing date, source, project, path, first prompt snippet, and match snippets (type-tagged: text, command, file, error) with color formatting @spec @cli-search @implemented
- cli > the search command exits with an error if no query is provided (positional argument is required) @spec @cli-search @implemented
- label: cli > the messages command takes a session file path as positional argument and outputs the session as a JSON messages array to stdout
  id: m1a
  tags: [spec, cli-messages, implemented]
- label: cli > the messages command supports --format openai|anthropic (default: anthropic) to control the output message format
  id: m2b
  tags: [spec, cli-messages, implemented]
- label: cli > the messages command supports --pretty flag for formatted JSON output (default: compact single-line JSON)
  id: m3c
  tags: [spec, cli-messages, implemented]
- label: cli > the messages command is exported as cmdMessages from src/cli/cli-commands.js and re-exported from src/cli/index.js; bin/tracequest.js dispatches to it via case "messages"
  id: m6f
  tags: [spec, cli-messages, implemented]
- label: export > exportMessages includes session.systemPrompt when present: Anthropic format uses a top-level "system" field, OpenAI format prepends a role:"system" message
  id: m8h
  tags: [spec, cli-messages, implemented]
- label: cli > the messages command rejects directory inputs that are not Grok session directories (same validation as cmdRender)
  id: m9i
  tags: [spec, cli-messages, implemented]
- label: the find command treats non-numeric --limit values as context-dependent defaults (10 without --filter, 20 with --filter); search still defaults to 20
  id: m10j
  tags: [spec, cli-hardening, implemented]
- cli > the list command supports --limit N (-l) flag (default 20) to cap displayed results, using the same Number.isFinite guard pattern as cmdFind @spec @cli-list-limit @implemented
- cli > the messages command supports --out (-o) flag to write JSON output to a file instead of stdout; when used, writes a confirmation message to stderr @spec @cli-messages-out @implemented
- cli > the latest command supports --limit N (-l) flag (default 1); when N>1, it lists the top N latest sessions instead of rendering one, supporting --filter to filter before limiting @spec @cli-latest-limit @cli-filter @implemented
- the list, latest, find, and search commands treat non-numeric or negative --limit values as their respective defaults (20 for list/search, 10 without --filter and 20 with --filter for find, 1 for latest) @spec @cli-hardening @implemented
- CLI list, latest, share, and find use --filter (-f) for filter expressions; --expr is removed @spec @cli-filter @implemented
- the find command with no --filter and no positional args lists the 10 most recent sessions (findSessions mtime-desc order) and uses default --limit 10 @spec @cli-find @implemented
- label: CLI list with --sort recent and no --filter sorts discovered sessions by mtime without building the session index
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cmdList --sort recent without --filter avoids indexing' test/cli/cli-commands.test.js
  tags: [spec, cli-filter, cli-perf, implemented]
- label: CLI list with discovery-only filter expression fields and recent/date sort filters discovered sessions without building the session or search indexes
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cmdList discovery-only --filter avoids indexing' test/cli/cli-commands.test.js
  tags: [spec, cli-filter, cli-perf, implemented]
- label: CLI discovery-only filters evaluate from discovered metadata while avoiding session and search index builds
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cmdList compound discovery-only --filter avoids indexing' test/cli/cli-commands.test.js
  tags: [spec, cli-filter, cli-perf, implemented]
- label: CLI discovery-only filters reuse shared empty metadata when evaluating discovered sessions so index-free filtering does not allocate one placeholder meta object per session
  command: grep -q 'EMPTY_DISCOVERY_META' src/cli/cli-commands.js && TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cmdList compound discovery-only --filter avoids indexing' test/cli/cli-commands.test.js
  tags: [spec, cli-filter, cli-perf, anneal-empty-meta, implemented]
- label: CLI session commands accept a unique sessionHash anywhere they accept a session path and list/find/search outputs show the hash for each session
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/cli/session-hash-cli.test.js
  tags: [spec, session-hash, implemented]
- label: CLI global help, no-args help, help subcommand, and --version finish without importing command implementations or starting live-reload watchers
  command: node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/bin/tracequest-startup.test.js
  tags: [spec, cli-startup, implemented]
- label: the find command combines positional and --filter filter expressions with AND, so sessions must match both expressions when both are supplied
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'combines positional and --filter expressions with AND' test/cli/cli-commands.test.js
  tags: [spec, cli-find, cli-filter, implemented]
- label: the browser-open command used by share --open and render --open is overridable via the TRACEQUEST_OPEN_CMD environment variable (a single executable path/name resolved via PATH); tests always set it to a recording stub so no test run ever launches a real browser, on any platform
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test test/share/cli-share.test.js 2>&1 | grep -q '# fail 0' && [ "$(grep -c 'xdg-open' src/cli/cli-commands.js)" = 1 ] && grep 'xdg-open' src/cli/cli-commands.js | grep -q TRACEQUEST_OPEN_CMD && [ "$(grep -c 'openInBrowser(' src/cli/cli-commands.js)" = 3 ] && grep -q TRACEQUEST_OPEN_CMD test/share/cli-share.test.js
  tags: [cli, test-hygiene, spec, implemented]
- label: the list, find, and latest commands format session mtimes with new Date(s.mtime) (the same hardened form search already uses), so a discovery row carrying an epoch-ms mtime renders as a date instead of throwing "s.mtime.toISOString is not a function" mid-output
  command: test "$(grep -c "new Date(s\.mtime)" src/cli/cli-commands.js)" = "4" && TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/cli/cli-mtime-handoff-path.test.js
  tags: [spec, cli-hardening, implemented]
- label: the list, find, latest, and search printers (text and --json) normalize each session project with normalizeProjectFolder, the same function /api/sessions uses via sessionToApiObject, so both surfaces print an identical project string for the same record and an absolute project path never leaks into CLI output
  command: ! grep -q "projectLabel(" src/cli/cli-commands.js && test "$(grep -c "normalizeProjectFolder(" src/cli/cli-commands.js)" = "7" && OUT="$(TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/cli/cli-api-project-parity.test.js)" && echo "$OUT" | grep -q "^# pass 4$" && echo "$OUT" | grep -q "^# fail 0$"
  tags: [spec, cli-hardening, api-parity, implemented]

## serve
- label: tracequest serve eagerly builds the session index at startup (before the HTTP listener accepts connections) so indexing progress is visible in the terminal immediately
  command: grep -q 'resolveSessionsAndIndex' src/server.js
  tags: [spec, index, implemented]

## search
- tracequest search with an empty or whitespace-only query exits with the usage error before discovering or indexing sessions @cli @implemented
