- label: buildIndex indexes 3000 synthetic Claude sessions under 1200ms (JS pipeline)
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-name-pattern 'buildIndex cold indexes 3000' test/sessions/index-search-pipeline-perf.test.js
  tags: [implemented]
- label: searchSessions first hit under 5ms/op on 3000-session indexed corpus
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-name-pattern 'searchSessions first hit' test/sessions/index-search-pipeline-perf.test.js
  tags: [implemented]
- label: indexOpenCode preserves OpenCode message-part metadata for firstPrompt, model, chapters, tools, token counts, errors, duration, and term frequencies when building stored index metadata
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/sessions/session-index-opencode.test.js
  tags: [spec, anneal-opencode-index-meta, implemented]
- label: session discovery helpers read Claude, Codex, Factory, and Grok directory listings with Dirent entries so non-session entries do not need stat calls
  command: node -e 'const s=require("fs").readFileSync("src/sessions/session-discovery.js","utf8"); const i=s.indexOf("function readDirEntries("); if(i<0) process.exit(1); if(!/readdirSync\([^)]*\{\s*withFileTypes:\s*true\s*\}/s.test(s.slice(i, i+600))) process.exit(1); const n=(s.match(/readDirEntries\(/g)||[]).length-1; if(n < 7) process.exit(1)'
  tags: [spec, anneal-discovery-dirents, implemented]
- label: searchSessions scans a JSONL session file at most once when collecting snippets for multiple matched query terms in the same result row, while preserving BM25 result ordering and snippet contents
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/sessions/bm25-search.test.js test/sessions/index-search-pipeline-perf.test.js
  tags: [spec, cli-perf, implemented]
- label: sessions expose an 8-character lowercase hex sessionHash derived from SHA-256 of the canonical session path
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/sessions/session-hash.test.js
  tags: [spec, session-hash, implemented]
- id: 80i
  label: the JS indexSession fails closed on a session source that is not one of the seven known sources (claude, codex, cursor, cursor-cloud, factory, grok, opencode): there is no catch-all fallback to indexClaudeJsonl, it throws UnknownSessionSourceError naming the source and session path, buildIndex aborts the whole batch on the serial, parallel-worker, and sidecar-failure fallback paths, and no fabricated all-zero entry is ever written to index.json; a session with no source at all still defaults to the claude indexer (JS twin of sidecar fact z9k)
  command: ! grep -q 'indexer ? indexer(session) : indexClaudeJsonl' src/sessions/session-index-core.js && grep -q 'throw new UnknownSessionSourceError(source, session.path)' src/sessions/session-index-core.js && out=$(TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/sessions/session-index-fail-closed.test.js 2>&1) && echo "$out" | grep -qE '^. pass 10$' && echo "$out" | grep -qE '^. fail 0$'
  tags: [spec, sessions-fail-closed-facts, implemented, cursor-cloud]
- label: buildIndex closes the lazily opened read-only OpenCode SQLite handle in a finally on both serial indexing paths (the in-process loop and indexStaleChunkSerial), so a batch that fails closed on an unrecognised source mid-loop does not leak one file descriptor per refused build in a long-lived serve process
  command: test "$(grep -c "if (opencodeDb) opencodeDb.db.close();" src/sessions/index-writers.js)" = "2" && test "$(grep -cE "^ *\} finally \{$" src/sessions/index-writers.js)" -ge 2 && out=$(TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/sessions/index-writers-opencode-db-leak.test.js 2>&1) && echo "$out" | grep -qE "^. pass 1$" && echo "$out" | grep -qE "^. fail 0$"
  tags: [spec, sessions-fail-closed-facts, implemented]

## live
- label: live session probes ignore expected /proc race errors while still logging unexpected probe failures
  command: node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/sessions/live-sessions.test.js
  tags: [spec, anneal-live-proc-errors, implemented]

# project
- label: tracequest is a zero-dependency ESM Node.js CLI that transforms agent session logs into interactive HTML visualizations
  command: test -f package.json && grep -q "\"type\": \"module\"" package.json

# release
- label: README.md documents install from a clean clone (git clone, npm install) and carries no `npm install -g tracequest` registry command, since the package is not published to a registry
  command: grep -q "git clone https://github.com/viktor-com/tracequest" README.md && ! grep -qE "npm install -g tracequest" README.md
  tags: [implemented, release]
- label: `tracequest serve` listens on 127.0.0.1 by default and only on another address when `--bind <addr>` is passed, because its API can launch and kill agent runs and has no authentication
  command: node --test test/server/server-bind.test.js
  tags: [spec, release, security]
- label: the project root contains the MIT LICENSE held by av, and package.json declares "license": "MIT"
  command: grep -q "^MIT License" LICENSE && grep -q "Copyright (c) 2026 av" LICENSE && grep -q "\"license\": \"MIT\"" package.json
  tags: [release, license]
- label: package.json contains repository, homepage, bugs, and author metadata, and is marked "private": true so the complete tarball is never publishable to a registry
  command: node -e "const p=require(\"./package.json\"); for (const k of [\"repository\",\"homepage\",\"bugs\",\"author\"]) if(!p[k]) throw new Error(k); if(p.private!==true) throw new Error(\"private\")"
  tags: [implemented, release]
- the project root contains a CHANGELOG.md documenting changes for the current and prior versions following a standard format @implemented
- a .github/workflows directory exists with CI configuration that at minimum runs facts check and basic validation on pushes and pull requests @implemented
- package.json defines a 'test' script and the project includes tests that exercise core parse, render, and sessions modules @implemented
- the CLI supports --version (or -v) that prints the version from package.json and the CLI is updated when package version changes @implemented
- label: package.json contains a "files" field that whitelists runtime code and assets ("bin/", "src/", "data/", "presets/") plus release documentation ("README.md", "LICENSE", "THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md", "CHANGELOG.md", "CONTRIBUTING.md", "SECURITY.md", "package.json") resulting in a clean tarball; `npm pack --dry-run` succeeds with no extraneous file warnings
  command: npm pack --dry-run 2>&1 | grep -qv "npm warn" && npm pack --dry-run >/dev/null 2>&1
  tags: [implemented, release]
- label: Release 0.1.2 publishes Cursor source support with package.json version and CHANGELOG entry
  command: node -e "const fs=require('fs'); const ch=fs.readFileSync('CHANGELOG.md','utf8'); if(!ch.includes('## [0.1.2] - 2026-07-05')) throw new Error('missing changelog 0.1.2'); if(!/Cursor/.test(ch)) throw new Error('missing Cursor changelog entry');"
  tags: [spec, release, cursor, implemented]
- label: the project root contains THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md retaining the upstream MIT copyright and permission notice for the vendored gitleaks ruleset shipped as data/secret-rules.json
  command: test -f THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md && grep -q "Zachary Rice" THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md && grep -q "gitleaks" THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md && grep -q "THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md" package.json
  tags: [release, license]
- label: the package is unscoped ("tracequest"), marked "private": true, and has no publishConfig: it is distributed as a signed-off tarball, never published to a registry
  command: node -e "const p=require(\"./package.json\"); if(p.name!==\"tracequest\") throw 1; if(p.private!==true) throw 2; if(p.publishConfig) throw 3; if(!p.bin?.tracequest) throw 4"
  tags: [release, license]
- label: a prepack preflight aborts `npm pack` when the LICENSE is not the MIT License, THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md is missing, or the package is not marked private; a prepublishOnly hook unconditionally aborts `npm publish`
  command: test -f scripts/preflight-dist.mjs && node -e "const p=require(\"./package.json\"); if(!/preflight-dist/.test(p.scripts.prepack||\"\")) throw 1; if(!/preflight-dist/.test(p.scripts.prepublishOnly||\"\")) throw 2" && node scripts/preflight-dist.mjs && ! node scripts/preflight-dist.mjs --forbid-publish
  tags: [release, license]
- label: CONTRIBUTING.md welcomes contributions under the MIT License (inbound = outbound, no copyright assignment) and points to CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
  command: grep -q "MIT" CONTRIBUTING.md && grep -q "CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md" CONTRIBUTING.md && ! grep -qi "proprietary" CONTRIBUTING.md && test -f CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
  tags: [release, license]
- label: sidecar executable detection lives in src/sessions/native-executable.js and recognizes ELF, Mach-O (incl. universal) and PE, so scripts/ensure-sidecar.mjs and detectSidecar share one gate and neither re-runs cargo nor rejects a valid binary
  command: grep -q "0xcf" src/sessions/native-executable.js && grep -q "0x7f" src/sessions/native-executable.js && grep -q "native-executable.js" scripts/ensure-sidecar.mjs && grep -q "native-executable.js" src/sessions/index-writers.js && ! grep -rq "isElfExecutable" src/ scripts/
  tags: [release, sidecar]
- label: the sidecar gate accepts any native executable format (ELF, Mach-O incl. universal, PE), so a release sidecar built on macOS or Windows is used instead of being rejected as non-ELF and silently falling back to JS
  command: node -e "import(\"./src/sessions/native-executable.js\").then(m=>{var p=\"sidecar/bin/\"+process.platform+\"-\"+process.arch+\"/tracequest-sidecar\"; var t=m.readExecutableTarget(p); if(!t||t.arch!==process.arch) throw new Error(\"host sidecar rejected\")})" && ! grep -q "isElfExecutable" src/sessions/index-writers.js
  tags: [release, sidecar]
- label: distribution is one tarball per platform: sidecar/bin/ holds exactly the target platform directory, package.json ships sidecar/bin/, detectSidecar prefers it, and a host/tarball platform mismatch warns instead of silently falling back
  command: node -e "const f=require(\"./package.json\").files; if(!f.includes(\"sidecar/bin/\")) throw new Error(\"files\")" && grep -q "sidecar/bin" src/sessions/index-writers.js && grep -q "bundles the sidecar for" src/sessions/index-writers.js && test -f scripts/dist-platforms.mjs && test -f scripts/pack-dist.mjs
  tags: [release, sidecar]
- label: the prepack preflight refuses to build a tarball unless sidecar/bin holds exactly one platform, matching the tarball target, whose binary CPU architecture read from its Mach-O/ELF header matches its directory name
  command: node scripts/preflight-dist.mjs >/dev/null && grep -q "readExecutableTarget" scripts/preflight-dist.mjs && grep -q "expected only" scripts/preflight-dist.mjs
  tags: [release, sidecar]
- label: Security reporting uses the repository private advisory channel and project author metadata uses the public repository owner handle
  id: pubct
  command: node --test test/portability.test.js
  tags: [spec, portability, implemented]

# export
- The /markdown export route (and generateMarkdown) returns well-formed text/markdown (200 + attachment) for valid session paths under allowed agent dirs (with isSessionPath guard); returns 400 for missing path, 403 for non-session paths (incl !exists or outside ~/.claude etc); never 200 garbage or uncaught crash on bad input (post path validation + parse hardening). @implemented
- Markdown export (generateMarkdown + buildMarkdownChapters) applies safeSlice + line caps (e.g. prompts to first 10 lines + '...', responses 800 chars, cmds 12, diffs 8, etc) with '*...and N more*' notes; handles long/unicode/special-char/empty prompts, command outputs, and assistant text from real + adversarial sessions without truncation errors, RangeError, or mojibake in the emitted .md. @implemented
- The /markdown export emits user prompts, assistant responses, tool outputs/diffs/commands etc as raw text inside MD structures ( > blockquotes, bare after ###, ``` fences, `code` ); <>&" and MD-significant chars from data are preserved (no html esc, minimal md-escape); export never crashes and produces valid UTF8 .md for sessions containing them or 'no thanks'/'node version' correction phrases (chapter text does not affect export outcomes). @implemented
- buildMarkdownChapters (md export only) sets chapter outcome exclusively from error counts (0=clean, 1-2=corrected, >=3=struggling); never inspects next-prompt text for correction hints (no equivalent to render's /\b(no|wrong|...)\b|not what/i ); therefore sessions/chapters with "no thanks", "wrong", "instead", "node version" etc produce no "corrected" flag or "outcome: corrected" in .md (even when renderHTML UI does post-iter4 fix); chapter flags in exported markdown can differ from HTML view for same session. @implemented
- The /markdown route in server.js continues to call generateMarkdown imported from ./markdown.js; the response (status codes, headers, attachment filename, exported content) is identical for all valid and invalid inputs @implemented
- Markdown export functions (generateMarkdown, buildMarkdownChapters, detectGitOpMd, fmtTokens, formatDuration, fmtDurationMd) and the shared fmtMcpName helper live in src/markdown.js; server.js imports generateMarkdown and fmtMcpName from ./markdown.js and no longer contains their definitions inline @implemented
- label: export > exportMessages(session, { format }) in src/export/messages-export.js converts a parsed Session to Anthropic format: user turns have role "user" with text content, assistant turns have role "assistant" with text and tool_use content blocks, tool results become role "user" messages with tool_result content blocks
  id: m4d
  tags: [spec, cli-messages, implemented]
- label: export > exportMessages with format "openai" converts tool_use blocks to tool_calls array with function name/arguments on assistant messages, and tool results become separate messages with role "tool" and tool_call_id
  id: m5e
  tags: [spec, cli-messages, implemented]
- label: export > exportMessages is exported from src/api/tracequest-api.js for programmatic use
  id: m7g
  tags: [spec, cli-messages, implemented]

# render-ui
- id: 4tr
  label: render-assemble.js builds CHAPTERS_HELPERS_JS from CHAPTERS_BUNDLE_PARTS and inserts it into UI_BUNDLE_PARTS before CHAPTERS_JS and UI_CHAPTERS_JS, keeping chapter helpers in browser scope before the chapter UI runs
  command: node --input-type=module -e 'import { UI_BUNDLE_PARTS, CHAPTERS_HELPERS_JS, CHAPTERS_BUNDLE_FUNCTION_NAMES } from "./src/render/render-assemble.js"; const ids = UI_BUNDLE_PARTS.map((p) => p.id); const helpers = ids.indexOf("chapters-helpers"), chapters = ids.indexOf("chapters"), ui = ids.indexOf("ui-chapters"); if (!(helpers >= 0 && helpers < chapters && chapters < ui)) process.exit(1); for (const name of CHAPTERS_BUNDLE_FUNCTION_NAMES) if (!CHAPTERS_HELPERS_JS.includes("function " + name)) process.exit(1);'
  tags: [render-ui-facts, implemented]
- id: th2
  label: RENDER_BUNDLE_PARTS embeds the same UI_BUNDLE_PARTS between CORE_JS and MAIN_JS, so renderHTML splices the pre-built RENDER_JS bundle instead of rebuilding viewer script parts at runtime
  command: node --input-type=module -e 'import { RENDER_BUNDLE_PARTS, UI_BUNDLE_PARTS, RENDER_JS } from "./src/render/render-assemble.js"; const ids = RENDER_BUNDLE_PARTS.map((p) => p.id); const core = ids.indexOf("core"), main = ids.indexOf("main"); if (!(core >= 0 && main > core)) process.exit(1); for (const part of UI_BUNDLE_PARTS) { const i = ids.indexOf(part.id); if (!(i > core && i < main)) process.exit(1); } for (const name of ["render", "buildChapters", "getChapters", "renderFilterBar"]) if (!RENDER_JS.includes("function " + name)) process.exit(1);'
  tags: [render-ui-facts, implemented]
- id: 9vv
  label: tracequest render --out and tracequest serve /view both deliver renderHTML output whose browser script defines getChapters/renderFilterBar before first render and contains no premature script end tag
  command: node --input-type=module -e 'import { readFileSync } from "node:fs"; const cli = readFileSync("src/cli/cli-commands.js", "utf8"); const routes = readFileSync("src/routes/route-handlers-pages.js", "utf8"); if (!cli.includes("renderHTML(session)") || !routes.includes("mods.renderHTML(session)")) process.exit(1);' && TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'renderHTML viewer script block contains bootstrap|renderHTML splices pre-assembled RENDER_JS with chapter list UI' test/render/render-assemble.test.js test/render-ui-chapters.test.js
  tags: [render-ui-facts, implemented]

# browser-page
- browserPage (the data-transformation function that maps sessions+index into serialized data and calls browserPageHTML) is defined in src/browser-page.js and exported alongside browserPageHTML; server.js imports browserPage from ./browser-page.js and no longer contains the data-transformation function inline @implemented
- browser-client computeGrade and estimateCost cache their results on the session object after the first call, eliminating redundant recalculation during filter, sort, and render operations @implemented
- browser-client matchTerm uses pre-lowercased _searchLower, _projectLower, and _modelLower fields on session objects instead of calling toLowerCase() on every filter evaluation, eliminating redundant string operations during filtering @implemented
- browser-client qfMatchSession uses pre-lowercased _promptLower, _idLower, _modelLower, and _projectLower fields on session objects instead of calling toLowerCase() on every quick-filter evaluation, eliminating redundant string operations in the quick-filter bar @implemented
- browser-page browserPage does not LRU-cache HTML output (buildPageCacheKey removed); each handleIndex call runs browserPage while resolveSessionsAndIndex in route-cache may skip findSessions and buildIndex on unchanged data @implemented
- browser-page browserPageHTML splits its constant HTML template into module-scope HTML_HEAD, HTML_MIDDLE, and HTML_TAIL strings, assembling only the dynamic filterVal and browserClientScript(data) portions on each cache miss, eliminating redundant per-call allocation of the ~800-line constant template literal @implemented
- browser-client browserClientScript splits its constant JavaScript template into a module-scope BROWSER_CLIENT_SCRIPT_TAIL string, assembling only the dynamic var ALL = data portion on each call, eliminating redundant per-call allocation of the ~1200-line constant template literal @implemented
- browser-page buildPageCacheKey removed from src/browser/browser-page.js; route-cache buildRouteCacheKey (filter, modVersion, indexDiskMtimeMs, data-root mtimes) keys resolveSessionsAndIndex instead @implemented
- browser-page buildPageCacheKey XOR mtime checksum removed with page HTML LRU; route-cache isRouteCacheTopMtimesValid statSyncs top 5 cached sessions to invalidate stale session/index data @implemented
- updatePageUrl persists sort, expr (filter expression), grade, errorsOnly, source, and model in the URL query string alongside page and pageSize; on page load, a restore-from-URL IIFE reads these params back into currentSort, filterInput.value, and qfState before buildQfBar/render, and triggers a fetchSessions re-fetch if any filter/sort state was present in the URL (bypassing the inlined first-page data) @spec @implemented

# routes
- handleIndex, handleView, handleExport, handleMarkdown, and handleCompare in src/routes.js prefix their unused req parameter with _req to signal intent; the Node.js HTTP handler signature is preserved and behavior is unchanged @implemented
- In src/routes.js handleIndex, the redundant intermediate variable qf is eliminated; url.searchParams.get('filter') is used directly in the filter expression @implemented
- routes handleIndex uses route-cache resolveSessionsAndIndex (buildRouteCacheKey) to skip redundant findSessions and buildIndex when session data is unchanged; browserPage re-renders HTML on every request (no page-level HTML cache) @implemented
- routes handleIndex includes a supplementary newest-session mtime check that statSyncs the newest cached session file and compares its current mtime against the cached newestMtime, invalidating the route cache when the file has been modified, compensating for Linux directory mtime behavior that does not update when existing files are modified @implemented
- routes handleIndex supplementary mtime check statSyncs the top 5 cached session files and compares each current mtime against cached topMtimes values, invalidating the route cache if any of the top 5 sessions have been modified, catching stale data from ongoing writes to recently-active sessions beyond just the newest one @implemented

# markdown
- In src/markdown.js detectGitOpMd, the intermediate variable c (let c = cmd.trim()) is eliminated; cmd.trim() is used directly in the parts declaration, and no let declaration exists in detectGitOpMd @implemented
- In src/markdown.js generateMarkdown, the redundant single-use intermediate variables label (d.name === 'Write' ? 'write' : 'edit') and status (cmd.ok ? 'ok' : 'FAIL') are inlined into their sole call sites; no const label or const status declarations remain in the diffs and commands sections @implemented

# features

## live
- label: browser session index renders every external live session (liveSessions minus run-linked recordings) as a unified first-class live row pinned at the top of the session list — the SAME row treatment launched runs get: run-state-badge 'running', source pill, id, project, optional model, first prompt, and a muted 'external' origin chip — whose single click target is the read-only live chat at /run?session=<sessionHash>; the separate Live strip (#liveBar) is gone
  command: node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/browser/browser-client-dashboard.test.js
  tags: [spec, live-ui, implemented]
- label: browser session rows display a live indicator badge reading 'running' (the one status vocabulary shared with run rows) when s.live === true, so paginated result pages still identify generating sessions without a second livePaths fallback
  command: node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/browser/browser-client-dashboard.test.js
  tags: [spec, live-ui, implemented]
- label: the serve API populates liveSessions from the full unfiltered session snapshot and the browser refresh path updates the pinned unified live rows and row badge set from each /api/sessions response
  command: node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/routes/route-handlers-pages.test.js test/browser/browser-client-dashboard.test.js test/bin/tracequest-serve-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, live-ui, implemented]
- label: CLI reference serve docs mention live running sessions and the live:true filter key
  command: grep -q 'live running sessions' docs/cli-reference.md && grep -q 'live:true' docs/cli-reference.md
  tags: [spec, live-ui, docs, implemented]
- label: served browser tests load tracequest serve in Playwright and verify an external live session renders as ONE unified live row (run-row chrome, running state badge, external origin chip, /run?session=<hash> click target, no #liveBar strip, no duplicate session row) from the initial page payload and after window._refreshData fetches /api/sessions
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/browser/live-ui-served.playwright.test.js
  tags: [spec, live-ui, browser-live, implemented]
- label: served browser tests verify a real data-update SSE event from tracequest serve, triggered by writing a watched session file, causes the open browser page to fetch /api/sessions and render the new session without directly calling window._refreshData
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'data-update SSE refreshes the open page without direct refresh invocation' test/browser/live-ui-served.playwright.test.js
  tags: [spec, live-ui, browser-live, serve-refresh, implemented]

## filter
