# cursor
- label: Cursor sessions are a first-class Source; parseSession auto-detects .cursor/ paths and parseSession(path, "cursor") preserves source=cursor while using Claude-family JSONL event semantics
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cursor source preserves cursor identity|parseSession returns unified shape for all supported sources' test/parse.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor, implemented]
- label: findSessions and discoveryRoots include ~/.cursor/projects, and Cursor discovery appends .jsonl session rows with source=cursor, project labels, size, and mtime using the Claude-family directory layout
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cursor root is .cursor/projects under HOME|findSessions discovers sessions from minimal provider directory trees|discovers cursor jsonl in project directory|filesystem discovery helpers use Dirent traversal and shared output arrays' test/sessions/discovery-roots.test.js test/sessions.test.js test/sessions/session-discovery.test.js test/sessions/session-discovery-source-contracts.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor, implemented]
- id: 2w4
  label: Cursor indexing, peek metadata, and snippet scanning use the same Claude-family tokenizer input and query-scan behavior while preserving source=cursor in search results, and the multi-source search/serve integration covers all seven agent sources
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'source indexers tokenize source content into per-session termFreqs|cursor source routes to peekCursor|cursor snippets scan Claude-family JSONL content|search and serve API return hits from all seven agent sources' test/sessions/search-tokenizer-source-contract.test.js test/sessions/peek-session-dispatch.test.js test/sessions/scan-queries.test.js test/bin/tracequest-multi-source-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor, search, implemented, cursor-cloud]
- label: Cursor session paths under ~/.cursor/ are accepted by session routes and served UI surfaces show Cursor with a dedicated source color in browser rows, compare cards, and rendered session headers
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'cursor paths under home are accepted as session paths|SOURCE_COLORS includes cursor|source badge uses palette color for cursor|renderHeader shows source badge for cursor agents' test/server/server-session-path.test.js test/browser/browser-client.test.js test/browser/compare-page.test.js test/render/render-core-shell.test.js
  tags: [spec, cursor, ui, implemented]
- id: xxd
  label: README, tests/README.md, and multi-source integration docs describe Cursor as a supported source and the multi-source harness verifies all seven source labels
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'multi-source integration spec execution results match automated scenarios' test/browser/browser-verification-targets.test.js && grep -q 'Cursor' README.md && grep -q 'seven-source' tests/multi-source-integration.md
  tags: [spec, cursor, docs, implemented, cursor-cloud]
- label: When a Cursor transcript row carries no explicit token usage, indexCursorJsonl and Rust index_cursor estimate tokens factory-style: inputTokens = round(user-prompt chars / 4), outputTokens = round((assistant text chars + per tool_use name length + 20) / 4), totalTokens = their sum; explicit usage fields, when present, take precedence over estimation
  command: node --test --test-name-pattern 'estimates tokens, defaults model' test/sessions/session-index-family.test.js 2>&1 | grep -q '^# pass 1'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: Real-format Cursor sessions produce a nonzero estimateCost result from their estimated tokens, so cost surfaces for Cursor in dashboard stats, compare cards, markdown export, share metadata, and --sort cost exactly like factory and grok sessions
  command: node scripts/facts/check-cursor-cost.mjs
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: Cursor sessions resolve their model from Cursor's state.vscdb (composerData:<uuid> -> modelConfig.modelName) consistently in parseCursor, indexCursorJsonl, peekCursor, and Rust index_cursor; when the DB or the row is absent all four fall back to 'cursor' (Cursor's JSONL transcript itself carries no model field)
  command: node scripts/facts/check-cursor-model.mjs && grep -q 'cursor_model_from_state_db(path)' sidecar/src/indexer.rs
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: Cursor turn_ended rows with status 'error' increment the session error count in indexCursorJsonl and Rust index_cursor, and parseCursor emits an error tool_result event carrying the row's error text so parsed stats.errors matches the index
  command: node --test --test-name-pattern 'turn_ended' test/sessions/session-index-family.test.js 2>&1 | grep -q '^# fail 0' && node --test --test-name-pattern 'cursor identity' test/parse.test.js 2>&1 | grep -q '^# fail 0'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: Cursor file tracking counts the 'path' input of Read, Write, StrReplace, and Delete tool_use blocks (Cursor uses 'path' where Claude uses 'file_path'), identically in indexCursorJsonl and Rust index_cursor
  command: node --test --test-name-pattern 'touched file' test/sessions/session-index-family.test.js 2>&1 | grep -q '^# pass 1'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: buildSession returns null startTime and endTime when no event carries a timestamp, so Cursor session headers show a dash instead of the 1970-01-01 epoch date; durationMs stays 0
  command: node --test --test-name-pattern 'null' test/parse/parse-session-build.test.js 2>&1 | grep -q '^# fail 0'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: parseCursor sets sessionId to the transcript file's basename UUID (Cursor stores each session at agent-transcripts/<uuid>/<uuid>.jsonl)
  command: node --test --test-name-pattern 'cursor identity' test/parse.test.js 2>&1 | grep -q '^# fail 0'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: Cursor tool names normalize into the shared vocabulary: StrReplace maps to Edit (enabling diffInfo old/new rendering) and AskQuestion maps to Ask, in JS normalizeToolName and the Rust sidecar normalize_tool_name
  command: node -e "import('./src/parse/parse-enrich.js').then(m=>{if(m.normalizeToolName('StrReplace')!=='Edit'||m.normalizeToolName('AskQuestion')!=='Ask')process.exit(1)})" && grep -q 'strreplace.*Edit' sidecar/src/indexer.rs && grep -q 'askquestion.*Ask' sidecar/src/indexer.rs
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: enrichToolEvent Write diffInfo falls back to Cursor's 'contents' input key when 'content' is absent, so Cursor Write blocks render file content previews
  command: node -e "import('./src/parse/parse-enrich.js').then(m=>{const tc=m.enrichToolEvent({id:null,rawName:'Write',input:{path:'/a',contents:'hello'}});if(tc.diffInfo.content!=='hello')process.exit(1)})"
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: parseCursor extracts assistant thinking blocks into event.thinking (sliced to 2000 chars) exactly like parseClaude, matching the indexer which already tokenizes Cursor thinking content
  command: node --test --test-name-pattern 'cursor identity' test/parse.test.js 2>&1 | grep -q '^# fail 0'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: parseCursor derives cwd as the most frequent Shell tool_use input.working_directory value in the transcript (ties broken by first occurrence); when no working_directory appears, cwd stays null and the header shows a dash — no path-prefix guessing from Read/Write inputs
  command: node --test --test-name-pattern 'cursor identity|cursor derives cwd and gitBranch' test/parse.test.js 2>&1 | grep -q '^# fail 0'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: parseCursor derives gitBranch from the first SetActiveBranch tool_use input.branchName in the transcript, mirroring Claude's first-row-wins branch semantics; absent that, gitBranch stays null
  command: node --test --test-name-pattern 'cursor identity|cursor derives cwd and gitBranch' test/parse.test.js 2>&1 | grep -q '^# fail 0'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: parseCursor falls back to file timestamps when the transcript has no event timestamps: startTime = birthtime, endTime = mtime, durationMs = max(0, mtime - birthtime), with birthtime treated as missing when unavailable or later than mtime (then startTime = endTime = mtime, durationMs 0); the session carries timesEstimated: true
  command: node --test --test-name-pattern 'file timestamps|file-time' test/parse.test.js 2>&1 | grep -E '^# (pass 2|fail 0)' | wc -l | grep -q 2
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: rendered session headers prefix duration and started with '~' when session.timesEstimated is true, matching the existing '~' convention for estimated tokens
  command: test $(grep -c "timesEstimated ? '~' : ''" src/render/render-core-shell.js) -eq 2
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: JS indexCursorJsonl and Rust index_cursor derive durationMs identically from file birthtime/mtime with the same clamping rules as parseCursor, so list --sort duration ranks Cursor sessions
  command: node --test --test-name-pattern 'durationMs' test/sessions/session-index-family.test.js 2>&1 | grep -q '^# pass 2' && grep -q 'cursor_file_duration_ms(path)' sidecar/src/indexer.rs && grep -q 'cursorFileTimeBounds(filePath)' src/sessions/session-index-jsonl.js
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: chapter accumulation never keys tool-result or tool-name maps on a null/undefined toolUseId: a tool_result with null toolUseId renders as a standalone session-level error block, and a tool call with null id never resolves a result — so Cursor turn_ended errors cannot mark unrelated tool calls as failed in the session view or markdown export
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-name-pattern 'null toolUseId' test/chapters/session-chapters.test.js && TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-name-pattern 'cursor null-id' test/export/markdown-export.test.js
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: the waveform error flag matches a tool_result to an assistant turn only when both toolUseId and tool call id are non-null, so a Cursor session with one turn_ended error does not paint every tool-bearing turn as errored
  command: grep -q "r.toolUseId != null && e.toolCalls?.some(tc => tc.id != null && tc.id === r.toolUseId)" src/render/render-analytics-charts.js
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: in the compare view, duration, token, and cost rows treat a zero value as no-data: the cell shows a dash and neither side gets winner (delta-good) highlighting; when both sides have nonzero data the delta highlighting is unchanged; errors, files, and chapters rows keep existing semantics because zero is meaningful there
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-name-pattern 'null raw slots when value is zero|never wins against claude|zero-is-meaningful|zero raw slots \(no null coercion\)|skips winner classes when one raw slot is null' test/chapters/compare-metrics.test.js 2>&1 | grep -Eq '^# pass 5'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: compare cells prefix values with '~' when the underlying session has tokensEstimated (token and cost rows) or timesEstimated (duration row), so estimated Cursor metrics are never presented as measured when compared against real Claude metrics
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-name-pattern "carry '~' prefix|does not leak '~'|keeps winner highlighting" test/chapters/compare-metrics.test.js 2>&1 | grep -Eq '^# pass 3'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: the session stats bar shows a dash instead of '~0' for input tokens when totalInputTokens is 0 and tokens are estimated
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-name-pattern 'zero estimated input tokens|measured zero-input display' test/render/render-core-shell.test.js 2>&1 | grep -Eq '^# pass 2'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: share metadata includes tokensEstimated and timesEstimated booleans so downstream consumers can distinguish estimated from measured metrics
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-name-pattern 'estimated flags for cursor sessions|assembles session fields' test/share/metadata.test.js 2>&1 | grep -Eq '^# pass 2'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: Cursor CallMcpTool tool calls carry mcpInfo (server, tool, params from input.server/input.toolName/input.arguments) and appear in the MCP section like Claude's mcp__ tools, with a server/tool summary instead of a raw JSON dump; parse and index toolCounts stay in parity
  command: out=$(node --test --test-name-pattern "CallMcpTool" test/parse/parse-enrich.test.js 2>&1); echo "$out" | grep -Eq "^# pass [1-9]" && echo "$out" | grep -q "^# fail 0" && node --test test/parse/parse-index-toolcounts-parity.test.js 2>&1 | grep -q "^# fail 0"
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: SemanticSearch, Delete, Await, and Ask tool calls render human-readable input summaries (query, path, task id, first question) instead of JSON dumps, and have TOOL_COLORS entries distinct from the plain-text fallback
  command: out=$(node --test --test-name-pattern "SemanticSearch, Delete, Await" test/parse/parse-enrich.test.js 2>&1); echo "$out" | grep -Eq "^# pass [1-9]" && echo "$out" | grep -q "^# fail 0" && grep -q "SemanticSearch:" src/render/render-core.js && grep -q "Delete:" src/render/render-core.js && grep -q "Await:" src/render/render-core.js && grep -q "Ask:" src/render/render-core.js
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: chapter file tracking counts Delete tool paths so the session-view file count matches the dashboard index file count for Cursor sessions
  command: node --test --test-name-pattern "Delete tool call adds its path" test/chapters/session-chapters.test.js 2>&1 | grep -q "^# pass 1"
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: opening a Cursor parent session attaches agentHistory from agent-transcripts/<uuid>/subagents/*.jsonl sidecars, like Claude parents: discoverAgentSidecarPaths recognizes .cursor/ session paths and the record parser handles the Cursor row shape (role + message.content, no timestamps)
  command: node --test --test-name-pattern 'cursor' test/server/agent-history.test.js test/server-helpers-fetch.test.js 2>&1 | grep -q '# fail 0'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: Cursor agentHistory records without timestamps degrade gracefully: entries keep file order (mtime as the sidecar-level ordering key), render without fabricated epoch times, and never break the merged Claude-shape history pipeline
  command: node --test --test-name-pattern 'cursor|null timestamps|mixed merge|mtime' test/server/agent-history.test.js test/agent-history-surface.test.js 2>&1 | grep -q '# fail 0'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: the waveform maps assistant turns to chapters and draws chapter divider lines by event ordinal when timestamps are absent, so user prompts without timestamps still segment the waveform and clicking a turn jumps to its actual chapter
  command: out=$(node --test --test-name-pattern 'ordinal|actual chapter' test/render/render-analytics-charts.test.js 2>&1); echo "$out" | grep -q '^# fail 0' && echo "$out" | grep -Eq '^# pass [1-9]'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: the waveform hover tooltip omits the time row when a turn has no timestamp instead of rendering the epoch clock time
  command: out=$(node --test --test-name-pattern 'tooltip omits time row' test/render/render-analytics-charts.test.js 2>&1); echo "$out" | grep -q '^# fail 0' && echo "$out" | grep -Eq '^# pass [1-9]'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: when a turn has no input-token data the waveform draws at a neutral mid brightness instead of the minimum 'fully uncached' alpha, so estimated-token Cursor sessions are not uniformly dimmed
  command: out=$(node --test --test-name-pattern 'neutral mid alpha|neutral cacheRatio' test/render/render-analytics-charts.test.js 2>&1); echo "$out" | grep -q '^# fail 0' && echo "$out" | grep -Eq '^# pass [1-9]'
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]
- label: tracequest messages export emits a matching tool_result block ('(no output recorded)') for every Cursor tool_use so exported sequences are API-replay-valid; the synthetic session-level error (tool_use_id null) is emitted as plain text content, never as a tool_result referencing nothing
  command: node --test --test-name-pattern 'pairing' test/export/messages-export.test.js
  tags: [cursor, parity, spec, implemented]

# serve
- label: a session source the JS indexer refuses never escapes as an unhandled rejection: every HTTP route that resolves an index (/api/sessions, /api/search, /) answers a clean 500 through createHandle rather than terminating the serve process or returning a silent empty 200, and the index-mtime coalesce guard is released on the throw path so later mtime reads are not frozen by a leaked memo
  command: out=$(TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/routes/unknown-source-http-containment.test.js 2>&1) && echo "$out" | grep -qE "^. pass 5$" && echo "$out" | grep -qE "^. fail 0$"
  tags: [spec, sessions-fail-closed-facts, serve, implemented]

# runs-surface
- label: the assembled Runs surface uses run/runs in user-facing chrome (overview, filter, empty/error, compare, flyout kicker) instead of mixing sessions
  command: node --input-type=module -e "import { browserPageHTML } from \"./src/browser/browser-page-build.js\"; import { readFileSync } from \"node:fs\"; const h = browserPageHTML(JSON.stringify({sessions:[],total:0,page:1,pageSize:50,stats:{},liveSessions:[]}), \"\"); const need = [\"aria-label=\\\"Filter runs\\\"\",\"Select 2 runs to compare\",\"aria-label=\\\"Run analytics\\\"\",\"class=\\\"session-flyout-kicker\\\">Run</span>\",\"Close run analytics\",\"title=\\\"Run analytics\\\"\"]; for (const n of need) { if (!h.includes(n)) { console.error(\"missing \"+n); process.exit(1); } } if (h.includes(\"Filter sessions\") || h.includes(\"Session analytics\") || h.includes(\"Select 2 sessions\")) { console.error(\"stale sessions copy on Runs surface\"); process.exit(1); } const dash = readFileSync(\"src/browser/browser-client-dashboard.js\",\"utf8\"); if (!dash.includes(\"dashStat(totalSessions, 'runs')\")) { console.error(\"overview stat not runs\"); process.exit(1); } const client = readFileSync(\"src/browser/browser-client.js\",\"utf8\"); if (!client.includes(\"No runs match\") || !client.includes(\"Failed to load runs\")) { console.error(\"empty/error still sessions\"); process.exit(1); }"
  tags: [spec, ui, runs-home, filters-stats, session-flyout, runs-surface, implemented]
- label: the Runs heading shares the app bar 16px gutter so header, toolbar, stats, and table start on one left edge
  command: node --input-type=module -e "import { browserPageHTML } from \"./src/browser/browser-page-build.js\"; const h = browserPageHTML(JSON.stringify({sessions:[],total:0,page:1,pageSize:50,stats:{},liveSessions:[]}), \"\"); if (!h.includes(\"padding: 10px 16px 56px\")) { console.error(\"runs-home padding does not share app-top 16px gutter\"); process.exit(1); } if (/\\.container\\.runs-home \\{[\\s\\S]*?padding: 10px 20px 56px/.test(h)) { console.error(\"still 20px runs-home gutter\"); process.exit(1); }"
  tags: [spec, ui, runs-home, filters-stats, runs-surface, implemented]

# session-flyout
- label: /view?embed=1 hides the standalone viewer title/actions so the flyout header is the identity
  command: node --input-type=module -e "import { readFileSync } from \"node:fs\"; const pages = readFileSync(\"src/routes/route-handlers-pages.js\",\"utf8\"); const rules = readFileSync(\"src/render/render-session-rules.js\",\"utf8\"); for (const src of [pages, rules]) { if (!src.includes(\"body.embed-view .header-top\") || !src.includes(\"display: none\")) { console.error(\"embed-view still shows standalone header-top\"); process.exit(1); } }"
  tags: [spec, ui, session-flyout, runs-surface, implemented]
- label: Escape, J, and K typed in the embed-view iframe still close or step the Runs flyout
  command: grep -q "tq-flyout" src/render/render-core.js && grep -q "tq-flyout" src/browser/browser-client.js && grep -q "postMessage" src/render/render-core.js
  tags: [spec, ui, session-flyout, runs-surface, implemented]

# chat-home
- label: GET / serves the /run chat surface (session rail + conversation column + composer), not the Runs inventory table
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern "chat-home: / is the chat surface" test/browser/chat-home.test.js test/routes/route-handlers-pages.test.js
  tags: [spec, chat-home, ui, implemented]
- label: GET / opens a session or run in that chat when one exists — a live launched run first, else a live external session, else the newest indexed session — so the default lands on an opened conversation
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern "chat-home: opens" test/routes/route-handlers-pages.test.js
  tags: [spec, chat-home, ui, implemented]
- label: GET / with an empty corpus still renders the chat shell (rail + empty conversation + composer) so the product default is never a table inventory
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern "chat-home: empty" test/browser/chat-home.test.js test/routes/route-handlers-pages.test.js
  tags: [spec, chat-home, ui, implemented]
- label: GET /?session= and GET /?id= serve the same chat pages as /run?session= and /run?id=
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern "chat-home: query" test/routes/route-handlers-pages.test.js
  tags: [spec, chat-home, ui, implemented]
- label: the previous Runs inventory remains at GET /sessions so All sessions and the inventory chrome stay reachable without being the product default
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern "chat-home: inventory" test/browser/chat-home.test.js test/routes/route-handlers-pages.test.js
  tags: [spec, chat-home, ui, implemented]
- label: the chat-home left pane first-paints as a session rail with the filter query box visible (#filterBar / #filterInput); time/source/filters/display, Overview stats, and grade/source/model chips stay in .rail-filters-extra and are CSS-hidden until the rail filter disclosure is opened
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern "chat-home: session rail first-paints" test/browser/chat-home.test.js
  tags: [spec, chat-home, ui, implemented]
- label: the session rail exposes a compact #railFilterToggle disclosure that reveals the extra previous-home filter stack (.rail-filters-extra) in the left pane without leaving chat; slash focuses the always-visible query
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern "chat-home: rail filter disclosure" test/browser/chat-home.test.js
  tags: [spec, chat-home, ui, implemented]

# sidebar-filters
- label: the chat-home session rail carries the previous-home filter query box (#filterBar / #filterInput) so filter expressions can be typed without leaving chat
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern "sidebar-filters: query" test/browser/sidebar-filters.test.js
  tags: [spec, sidebar-filters, ui, implemented]
- label: the chat-home session rail exposes time-range (#ageBtn), Source (#sourceBtn), Filters (#filtersToggle), and Display (#displayToggle) controls
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern "sidebar-filters: toolbar" test/browser/sidebar-filters.test.js
  tags: [spec, sidebar-filters, ui, implemented]
- label: the chat-home session rail shows corpus overview stats (#railOverview) for live, runs, cost, tokens, cache, duration, errors, commits, files, and chapters
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern "sidebar-filters: overview" test/browser/sidebar-filters.test.js
  tags: [spec, sidebar-filters, ui, implemented]
- label: the chat-home session rail carries previous-home quick-filter chips (#qfBar) for grade, source, model, and errors, revealed by Filters
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern "sidebar-filters: chips" test/browser/sidebar-filters.test.js
  tags: [spec, sidebar-filters, ui, implemented]
- label: rail filters apply to the listed sessions: the rail fetches /api/sessions with expr and sort so the session list matches the filter
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern "sidebar-filters: apply" test/browser/sidebar-filters.test.js
  tags: [spec, sidebar-filters, ui, implemented]
- label: with sidebar filters present, GET / is still the chat surface (conversation + composer), not the Runs inventory table
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern "sidebar-filters: chat-surface|chat-home: / is the chat surface" test/browser/sidebar-filters.test.js test/browser/chat-home.test.js
  tags: [spec, sidebar-filters, ui, chat-home, implemented]
